BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Finding Recovery Through Exercise Skills and Hope (FRESH): Movies 
 Saturdays
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261219T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261219T163000
UID:6af37443899b61003312c446@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This program helps people stay active\, make friends\, learn n
 ew skills\, and have fun. Meet new folks\, build connections\, and tell us
  about programs and services you would like to see in the neighbourhood!\n
 Come and join the F.R.E.S.H. group for a Saturday screening of popular mov
 ies.\nWe'll be playing the following films\nDecember 5 – A Star is Born 
 (2018)\nDecember 12 – A.I. Artificial Intelligence\nDecember 19 – A Ch
 ristmas Story\nDrop in\, no registration is required.\nSnacks and refreshm
 ents are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will be available
  at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGerstein Crisis C
 entre's F.R.E.S.H. initiative\n.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, First floor\, Activity Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6af37443899b61003312c446
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Dining Room: In Search of the Perfect Dining Experience
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261210T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261210T150000
UID:6adbc7878a335b6ae5ca1800@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Best-selling author John Ota investigates the architecture\, i
 nterior design and dining experiences of historical significance. Ranging 
 from Claude Monet’s house\, to Lucy Maud Montgomery’s manse\, to Frida
  Kahlo’s Casa Azul\, to Downton Abbey\,\nThe Dining Room\nis an enlighte
 ning and joyous celebration of culture and the art of dining.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6adbc7878a335b6ae5ca1800
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
